I remember this type of problems are all about traversals


Same Tree / Symmetric Tree: recursión comparando dos árboles a la vez.
Invert Binary Tree: recursión que modifica estructura.
Path Sum: recursión acumulando un valor por el camino.
Lowest Common Ancestor: recursión que "sube" información.
Binary Tree Level Order Traversal: BFS con queue (cambia de recursión a iteración con cola).

# Binary Tree Cheatsheet

## Bounds approach (validate BST example)
Each node must fit inside a shrinking (min, max) range. Going left lowers the
ceiling to the current value; going right raises the floor.

```cpp
bool validate(TreeNode* root, long minVal, long maxVal) {
    if (!root) return true;
    if (root->val <= minVal || root->val >= maxVal) return false;
    return validate(root->left,  minVal, root->val) &&   // left: max = current
           validate(root->right, root->val, maxVal);     // right: min = current
}
// call: validate(root, LONG_MIN, LONG_MAX);
```
- Use when each node must respect limits from ALL ancestors, not just its parent.
- `long` (or nullptr pointers) avoids the INT_MIN/INT_MAX edge case.

---

## Compare two nodes at once (symmetric / same tree)
When the problem compares TWO structures, recurse with TWO node params.

```cpp
bool compare(TreeNode* a, TreeNode* b) {
    if (!a && !b) return true;     // both null  → match
    if (!a || !b) return false;    // one null   → mismatch
    return a->val == b->val
        && compare(/* ... */)
        && compare(/* ... */);
}
```
- Same Tree: parallel calls → `compare(a->left, b->left)` and `compare(a->right, b->right)`
- Symmetric Tree: crossed calls → `compare(a->left, b->right)` and `compare(a->right, b->left)`
- The null handling (first two ifs) replaces sentinels; no leaks, no value-range tricks.
- Use for: Same Tree, Symmetric Tree, Merge Two Trees, Leaf-Similar Trees.

